From: Trijal Saha <97483939+Trijal08@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] samsung-sit: spell out broadcast refusals like stock does

A config of accepted ranges alone is not the shape stock puts on the
wire. Across nineteen Android captures every SET_BROADCAST_SMS_CONFIG
frame also names the ranges the CP must refuse -- 4355, 4380-4382 and
4393-4395 carry selected = 0 in all of them, without exception -- so
writing only the accepts made us the one thing on the link shaped
differently.

ranges_like_stock() takes the ranges a caller wants accepted and adds
every default range they do not overlap, refused, sorted. Asking for
exactly stock's accepted set now reproduces stock's own eight entries,
which is the frame every captured boot ends on; the test asserts that.

Functionally the two are equivalent, since a range the CP was never told
about is not accepted either. This is for parity, and because the
negative entries are the only record of what was deliberately turned
off.

Also worth recording from that survey: stock's frame length does not
always follow 13 + 7*count. It sends 69 bytes for eight ranges, 76 for
the same eight with a spare zeroed slot, and 68 for seven with six pad
bytes. The CP keys off the count byte, not the length, so the exact form
here is accepted and so was a shorter one.

